1
00:01:29,990 --> 00:01:36,310
Hi, Mom! I'm home!

2
00:01:39,310 --> 00:01:41,910
What you cook in there? It smells amazing!

3
00:01:43,590 --> 00:01:45,350
It's your birthday, remember?

4
00:01:46,350 --> 00:01:48,650
I made all the recipes you love.

5
00:01:49,330 --> 00:01:50,750
Wow! You made them all?

6
00:01:51,010 --> 00:01:52,350
Every single dish.

7
00:01:53,010 --> 00:01:55,670
See? I whipped up all of your favorites.

8
00:01:56,390 --> 00:01:58,450
Fresh from the bells of the dungeon!

9
00:01:59,990 --> 00:02:04,250
Good morning. You okay?

10
00:02:05,570 --> 00:02:07,210
I had a nightmare.

11
00:02:10,150 --> 00:02:11,990
That's what I smelled in my nightmare!

12
00:02:12,430 --> 00:02:13,830
Can you guys smell that, too?

13
00:02:14,190 --> 00:02:16,850
Yep. The party across the way is making breakfast.

14
00:02:17,410 --> 00:02:18,830
They're having salted pork?

15
00:02:19,270 --> 00:02:20,410
On buns?

16
00:02:20,930 --> 00:02:22,050
It's not fair!

17
00:02:22,410 --> 00:02:23,170
It's horrible.

18
00:02:23,930 --> 00:02:25,630
Breaks my heart to see it.

19
00:02:25,630 --> 00:02:30,330
These days, the diet of young adventurers is a travesty.

20
00:02:30,810 --> 00:02:33,210
They only eat bread, dried meats, and wine.

21
00:02:33,730 --> 00:02:38,130
Look, I get it. Exploring dungeons takes stamina, which you build, by eating fatty meat.

22
00:02:38,430 --> 00:02:39,590
But fat ain't enough.

23
00:02:40,690 --> 00:02:42,910
Malnutrition is the real monster here.

24
00:02:42,910 --> 00:02:49,990
Now that I said my piece, I feel like a total hypocrite, because we have yet to eat a balanced meal.

25
00:02:50,890 --> 00:02:54,790
Elf girl, you know why you were drooling over that salted pork yonder?

26
00:02:55,670 --> 00:02:58,370
It's because your body's craving fat, that's why.

27
00:02:58,570 --> 00:03:00,270
It's because I don't want to eat monster!

28
00:03:00,710 --> 00:03:03,210
Today, we'll hunt monsters that are rich and fat.

29
00:03:03,650 --> 00:03:06,170
Honestly, it'd be nice if we could find one that laid eggs, too.

30
00:03:06,710 --> 00:03:09,590
Eggs are filling, nutritious, the perfect food.

31
00:03:10,010 --> 00:03:12,290
So, if you spot any eggs around, grab them.

32
00:03:13,050 --> 00:03:14,810
You thinking what I'm thinking?

33
00:03:15,230 --> 00:03:15,990
I am.

34
00:03:16,270 --> 00:03:17,110
Just say it.

35
00:03:17,670 --> 00:03:18,990
The body of a chicken.

36
00:03:19,690 --> 00:03:21,210
The tail of a snake.

37
00:03:21,770 --> 00:03:24,550
With spurs, sharp as knives, and loaded with venom.

38
00:03:25,830 --> 00:03:27,710
Basilisk, the king of snakes!

39
00:03:28,650 --> 00:03:30,150
All right, a basilisk.

40
00:03:30,810 --> 00:03:31,890
Isn't that great, Marcel?

41
00:03:31,890 --> 00:03:33,630
You'll get to eat chicken meat.

42
00:03:35,210 --> 00:03:36,130
Is it, though?

43
00:03:36,370 --> 00:03:38,370
Can you call it a chicken when it's half chicken?

44
00:03:38,790 --> 00:03:40,230
Man, what a cool concept.

45
00:03:40,790 --> 00:03:43,590
I always loved monsters that were a mix of more than one species.

46
00:03:44,070 --> 00:03:48,150
Something about mashing two species together elevates the appeal of each one.

47
00:03:48,490 --> 00:03:52,430
The basilisk makes you appreciate the things about a plain old chicken that make it special.

48
00:03:52,730 --> 00:03:54,950
A simple combo, but it really makes you think.

49
00:03:55,590 --> 00:03:58,650
By the way, here's a fact about the cockatrice and basilisk.

50
00:03:58,650 --> 00:04:00,370
Same family, different species.

51
00:04:00,370 --> 00:04:03,990
It's been my dream to do a taste test between the two.

52
00:04:04,550 --> 00:04:05,910
You got some weird dreams.

53
00:04:06,910 --> 00:04:09,270
There should be a basilisk nest here.

54
00:04:11,890 --> 00:04:12,670
There's one.

55
00:04:13,370 --> 00:04:15,130
The eggs look freshly laid, too.

56
00:04:15,130 --> 00:04:18,530
Let's bag them up before the folks come home.

57
00:04:19,050 --> 00:04:20,450
Are they supposed to be that long?

58
00:04:20,450 --> 00:04:21,170
Here, take it.

59
00:04:21,810 --> 00:04:23,750
But are they supposed to be this squishy?

60
00:04:24,570 --> 00:04:26,270
The cry of the basilisk.

61
00:04:26,530 --> 00:04:27,410
Come on, Senshi.

62
00:04:27,590 --> 00:04:30,150
I hope you wrapped yours in something soft like I did.

63
00:04:33,190 --> 00:04:34,650
What's the party with the grill?

64
00:04:34,650 --> 00:05:04,470
Hmm, bad escape technique.

65
00:05:04,710 --> 00:05:06,710
She's just asking to be kicked in the back.

66
00:05:06,970 --> 00:05:08,410
Would you just hurry up and save her?

67
00:05:17,150 --> 00:05:19,750
Spread your arms and legs out wide to look huge.

68
00:05:19,990 --> 00:05:21,690
Then do a crazy yell to sound threatening.

69
00:05:22,150 --> 00:05:23,710
It'll freak the thing out and keep it back.

70
00:05:23,710 --> 00:05:29,410
How about we pretend we don't know him?

71
00:05:30,230 --> 00:05:30,590
Stop!

72
00:05:37,330 --> 00:05:41,690
The basilisk has another head on its tail, so it seems like it has no blind spots.

73
00:05:41,890 --> 00:05:43,190
But it's still one body.

74
00:05:43,190 --> 00:05:47,870
So if you attack both heads at the same time, you'll split its tension in two directions.

75
00:05:49,430 --> 00:05:50,930
Confusing the whole body.

76
00:05:55,330 --> 00:05:56,310
How is he?

77
00:05:57,050 --> 00:05:58,250
Did he get poisoned?

78
00:05:58,710 --> 00:05:59,190
Yes.

79
00:05:59,650 --> 00:06:00,130
Laios!

80
00:06:00,690 --> 00:06:02,370
Senshi says he has an antidote.

81
00:06:02,790 --> 00:06:03,630
Let's have it, then.

82
00:06:04,390 --> 00:06:07,030
Actually, I was planning to use it for lunch today.

83
00:06:07,490 --> 00:06:07,930
Huh?

84
00:06:08,990 --> 00:06:09,850
He's dying!

85
00:06:10,130 --> 00:06:11,110
Can't you see that?

86
00:06:11,470 --> 00:06:15,030
Yeah, well, it's better to take one of these antidotes if it's cooked into something.

87
00:06:15,230 --> 00:06:16,530
Then hurry up and cook something!

88
00:06:17,390 --> 00:06:19,770
First, you gotta chop off the tail and feet.

89
00:06:20,250 --> 00:06:22,850
Parboil it for a sec, then pluck the feathers.

90
00:06:24,330 --> 00:06:24,810
Whoa!

91
00:06:25,550 --> 00:06:26,870
Looks like normal chicken.

92
00:06:27,510 --> 00:06:30,270
Next, you gotta pull out the guts and rub in some spices.

93
00:06:30,950 --> 00:06:34,170
Chop some vegetables up and cram them in with your medicinal herbs.

94
00:06:34,590 --> 00:06:35,790
Then tie up the cut.

95
00:06:36,350 --> 00:06:40,010
Last, jam a stick through the sucker and give it a good slow roasting.

96
00:06:41,050 --> 00:06:42,010
And it's ready.

97
00:06:42,310 --> 00:06:43,110
Hurry up!

98
00:06:43,190 --> 00:06:43,790
Give him some!

99
00:06:46,530 --> 00:06:51,750
It's so juicy and delicious.

100
00:06:52,550 --> 00:06:53,850
Sorry for the wait.

101
00:06:54,590 --> 00:06:56,170
Here, have some, too.

102
00:06:56,590 --> 00:06:57,470
I can't yet!

103
00:06:57,730 --> 00:06:59,330
I should cast a healing spell, too.

104
00:07:02,490 --> 00:07:03,610
Oh, my gosh.

105
00:07:03,650 --> 00:07:05,890
It's like something the dining hall serves.

106
00:07:06,070 --> 00:07:08,570
That probably does taste exactly like chicken meat.

107
00:07:08,830 --> 00:07:10,930
I heard snake meat tastes like chicken, too.

108
00:07:12,690 --> 00:07:13,570
I'm sorry.

109
00:07:13,850 --> 00:07:15,450
I'll cast that healing spell now.

110
00:07:16,030 --> 00:07:16,250
Oops.

111
00:07:16,250 --> 00:07:16,990
Now I'm ready.

112
00:07:18,410 --> 00:07:19,890
That was so good.

113
00:07:20,290 --> 00:07:22,510
It's the first decent meal I've had in the dungeon.

114
00:07:25,430 --> 00:07:31,050
Um, she and I, we've been trying for three months to get past this part of the dungeon.

115
00:07:31,770 --> 00:07:34,610
But the same monsters wipe us out every single time.

116
00:07:34,610 --> 00:07:40,050
So we thought, since you eat monsters, you could tell us how to get stronger so we can, too.

117
00:07:40,650 --> 00:07:41,350
Oh, sure.

118
00:07:41,350 --> 00:07:44,210
Um, first, improve your eating habits.

119
00:07:44,210 --> 00:07:46,230
Second, get enough sleep.

120
00:07:46,670 --> 00:07:47,910
Third, exercise.

121
00:07:48,710 --> 00:07:50,310
Follow these three rules.

122
00:07:50,310 --> 00:07:53,070
And in no time, you will get stronger.

123
00:07:53,070 --> 00:07:57,570
What an amazing group of adventurers.

124
00:07:57,570 --> 00:07:58,810
I want to be like them.

125
00:07:58,810 --> 00:08:07,790
I don't think we're going to make it to the third floor today.

126
00:08:07,790 --> 00:08:10,530
We spent too much time cooking that monster.

127
00:08:10,530 --> 00:08:14,390
They say red dragons wake up once a month to do their hunting.

128
00:08:14,890 --> 00:08:17,850
That means the one we ran into had just woken up.

129
00:08:18,050 --> 00:08:20,670
So it's probably asleep now, because its belly's full.

130
00:08:21,090 --> 00:08:24,150
I'd like to find it before it finishes digesting my sister.

131
00:08:24,150 --> 00:08:28,430
Hey, Marcel, would you please pick up the pace a little?

132
00:08:33,070 --> 00:08:34,750
Yeah, my sister's doomed.

133
00:08:35,750 --> 00:08:37,310
I'm not slowing us down.

134
00:08:37,550 --> 00:08:38,490
See, nothing's broken.

135
00:08:38,630 --> 00:08:39,290
I can walk.

136
00:08:39,390 --> 00:08:40,090
But you're hurt.

137
00:08:40,630 --> 00:08:41,550
We should take a break.

138
00:08:41,650 --> 00:08:42,530
I'm fine, really.

139
00:08:42,970 --> 00:08:43,790
Oh, please.

140
00:08:44,490 --> 00:08:46,250
Acting tough isn't helping one bit.

141
00:08:46,710 --> 00:08:50,130
You'll just end up really hurting yourself, and then you'll be a burden on the party.

142
00:08:52,090 --> 00:08:53,950
We could take the path outside.

143
00:08:54,150 --> 00:08:54,650
It's easier.

144
00:08:54,690 --> 00:08:57,150
Then we'll have to deal with those pesky big bats.

145
00:08:57,990 --> 00:09:00,170
I can take care of big bats with my magic!

146
00:09:00,310 --> 00:09:01,090
No, it's fine.

147
00:09:01,550 --> 00:09:03,370
We could take the secret path.

148
00:09:03,610 --> 00:09:05,510
There's lots of traps, but few monsters.

149
00:09:05,910 --> 00:09:07,430
I have spells that can disarm traps!

150
00:09:07,450 --> 00:09:08,230
Ah, don't bother.

151
00:09:08,430 --> 00:09:10,050
It's faster to let Chilchuk handle them.

152
00:09:10,390 --> 00:09:10,790
Oh.

153
00:09:11,590 --> 00:09:14,050
If we're taking a path with few monsters on it,

154
00:09:14,190 --> 00:09:16,910
we'll need to stock up on as much food as we can carry.

155
00:09:17,270 --> 00:09:19,170
Won't the leftovers from lunch be enough?

156
00:09:19,370 --> 00:09:20,790
That's mostly meat and eggs.

157
00:09:20,970 --> 00:09:22,510
We need some more vegetables, too.

158
00:09:22,510 --> 00:09:25,390
I know of a spot nearby where mandrakes grow.

159
00:09:25,630 --> 00:09:26,030
Oh!

160
00:09:26,250 --> 00:09:26,730
Oh!

161
00:09:27,130 --> 00:09:29,170
I know all about harvesting mandrakes!

162
00:09:29,390 --> 00:09:30,490
Leave everything to me!

163
00:09:31,810 --> 00:09:34,390
Mandrakes are basic ingredients for magical potions.

164
00:09:34,950 --> 00:09:36,970
And I'm the expert here in magic.

165
00:09:37,270 --> 00:09:39,590
So follow my lead and do exactly what I say.

166
00:09:40,050 --> 00:09:42,470
You can't just pull a mandrake out, because it'll scream.

167
00:09:42,710 --> 00:09:45,050
If you hear it, you'll either go insane or worse.

168
00:09:45,250 --> 00:09:45,570
Die.

169
00:09:45,570 --> 00:09:48,950
If we do this the wrong way, we'll lose time.

170
00:09:49,370 --> 00:09:51,170
And poor Fallon is pretty much doomed.

171
00:09:51,790 --> 00:09:53,150
So here's how we do this.

172
00:09:54,190 --> 00:09:57,210
First, we need one well-trained dog and one leash.

173
00:09:57,490 --> 00:09:58,130
A dog?

174
00:09:58,610 --> 00:10:00,750
Connect the dog to the mandrake with the leash.

175
00:10:01,110 --> 00:10:02,590
Call the dog from a safe distance.

176
00:10:03,390 --> 00:10:05,850
The dog will run to you and pull the mandrake out.

177
00:10:06,410 --> 00:10:07,770
What happens to the dog?

178
00:10:08,730 --> 00:10:09,650
It dies.

179
00:10:10,010 --> 00:10:10,850
That's horrible.

180
00:10:11,410 --> 00:10:13,950
And where are we supposed to get enough dogs to sacrifice?

181
00:10:14,630 --> 00:10:17,910
Wouldn't you say this plan is not only cruel, but totally inefficient?

182
00:10:18,290 --> 00:10:19,690
But that's what I was taught.

183
00:10:20,110 --> 00:10:22,190
Can't we just use a really long piece of rope?

184
00:10:22,350 --> 00:10:22,510
What?

185
00:10:22,650 --> 00:10:25,590
If we used a rope that's long enough so we couldn't hear the scream,

186
00:10:25,890 --> 00:10:27,310
we wouldn't need to kill a dog.

187
00:10:28,270 --> 00:10:30,710
Uh, there's a reason that won't work.

188
00:10:30,890 --> 00:10:33,570
Something about getting the right angle when you pull, or something.

189
00:10:33,570 --> 00:10:42,870
That was scary.

190
00:10:43,330 --> 00:10:44,210
Nice, Marcel.

191
00:10:45,730 --> 00:10:48,510
If you just cut their heads off first, they won't scream.

192
00:10:49,030 --> 00:10:49,850
Makes sense.

193
00:10:50,230 --> 00:10:51,770
But that's not the right way!

194
00:10:52,250 --> 00:10:53,890
I've been doing it this way for years.

195
00:10:54,330 --> 00:10:55,990
But this isn't like picking carrots.

196
00:10:56,070 --> 00:10:57,110
It's really dangerous.

197
00:10:57,650 --> 00:11:00,170
Senshi obviously has experience picking them.

198
00:11:00,170 --> 00:11:03,930
And speaking of, have you ever tried to do this like how your book's telling you?

199
00:11:05,010 --> 00:11:07,510
No, I felt sorry for the dog.

200
00:11:08,070 --> 00:11:11,150
Well, we don't have a dog, and we're wasting time talking about it.

201
00:11:11,270 --> 00:11:13,270
Sorry, but we're doing it Senshi's way.

202
00:11:13,990 --> 00:11:18,450
If such an easy method was safe, it would have been in the book and taught at school.

203
00:11:19,250 --> 00:11:23,670
What was I thinking, trying to get them to believe how the book says to pick a mandrake

204
00:11:23,670 --> 00:11:25,490
when I never tried that way before?

205
00:11:27,130 --> 00:11:28,670
That's right, I haven't.

206
00:11:28,670 --> 00:11:30,250
That settles it.

207
00:11:30,610 --> 00:11:32,890
I'll show them the book's method is the right way.

208
00:11:33,290 --> 00:11:34,250
Then they'll see.

209
00:11:34,670 --> 00:11:36,130
I'm so sorry, boys.

210
00:11:36,150 --> 00:11:37,070
I forgive you.

211
00:11:37,750 --> 00:11:40,030
Now what can I use instead of a dog?

212
00:11:40,350 --> 00:11:41,170
A monster!

213
00:11:42,110 --> 00:11:44,210
This floor is chock full of big bats.

214
00:11:44,650 --> 00:11:46,570
They should all be sleeping in their nests now.

215
00:11:49,010 --> 00:11:50,510
I hope this works.

216
00:11:50,890 --> 00:11:53,730
The book doesn't say how much strength it takes to yank these out.

217
00:11:53,730 --> 00:11:56,630
You know, it's a big bat nest by all the dung.

218
00:11:57,490 --> 00:12:00,250
I never thought Laios' trivia would come in handy.

219
00:12:00,870 --> 00:12:04,110
Now, I need to find somewhere far enough that I can't hear the scream.

220
00:12:04,690 --> 00:12:05,430
Like there!

221
00:12:05,430 --> 00:12:15,090
I can hit it from here easy.

222
00:12:17,430 --> 00:12:20,450
Aere fa'un orbit!

223
00:12:20,450 --> 00:12:31,510
Yes!

224
00:12:34,390 --> 00:12:36,350
Ow! What the heck?

225
00:12:36,910 --> 00:12:37,470
Marcel.

226
00:12:37,470 --> 00:12:50,490
Having experience is important.

227
00:12:51,310 --> 00:12:55,470
For example, now I know using a big bat instead of a dog is a stupid idea.

228
00:12:57,870 --> 00:12:58,290
Marcel!

229
00:12:59,650 --> 00:13:00,910
Is she dead?

230
00:13:00,910 --> 00:13:04,970
Oh, hey there.

231
00:13:07,150 --> 00:13:09,930
You used a big bat instead of a dog, huh?

232
00:13:10,230 --> 00:13:11,070
That's stupid.

233
00:13:13,090 --> 00:13:14,450
Hey, can you hear me?

234
00:13:14,750 --> 00:13:15,450
You okay there?

235
00:13:16,230 --> 00:13:18,470
Yeah, I can never feel better.

236
00:13:18,950 --> 00:13:19,550
Yeah, right.

237
00:13:20,610 --> 00:13:21,990
She heard the scream.

238
00:13:22,590 --> 00:13:24,810
Let's keep talking to her and it'll help clear her head.

239
00:13:26,410 --> 00:13:27,590
Why do you do that?

240
00:13:27,590 --> 00:13:30,770
When I heard my party call me a bird and I freak out.

241
00:13:31,190 --> 00:13:34,930
I do what I did to get back at him and make him grovel out of my foot.

242
00:13:35,230 --> 00:13:36,030
Why, you rotten!

243
00:13:36,210 --> 00:13:36,990
Take it easy.

244
00:13:37,450 --> 00:13:41,970
If I feel like I'm in a way and not helpful, it makes me real sad.

245
00:13:44,850 --> 00:13:47,430
The deeper we get in here, the tougher the monsters.

246
00:13:48,210 --> 00:13:50,290
We need your magic to keep us all safe.

247
00:13:50,950 --> 00:13:54,030
I don't want you wasting your power when we're just three floors down.

248
00:13:54,950 --> 00:13:57,090
We all have strengths and weaknesses.

249
00:13:57,090 --> 00:14:01,050
And when things get crazy, I'm going to need you to be ready.

250
00:14:01,490 --> 00:14:03,950
Till then, we can take care of everything else.

251
00:14:04,910 --> 00:14:06,530
We like to feel useful, too.

252
00:14:09,210 --> 00:14:10,150
Go on, Chillchuck.

253
00:14:10,450 --> 00:14:10,950
Tell her.

254
00:14:10,950 --> 00:14:14,110
I was wrong to say you were a burden.

255
00:14:14,670 --> 00:14:16,550
You've been a big help since day one.

256
00:14:21,550 --> 00:14:23,210
Say it like you mean it, shorty.

257
00:14:23,370 --> 00:14:26,410
I see you've got your lousy attitude back, you little...

258
00:14:26,410 --> 00:14:27,530
This is great.

259
00:14:27,970 --> 00:14:30,330
We snagged ourselves a nice, fat big bat.

260
00:14:30,670 --> 00:14:31,910
How's about we make some omelets?

261
00:14:33,590 --> 00:14:36,190
Are we 100% sure this thing's an egg?

262
00:14:36,390 --> 00:14:38,770
It looks nothing like any egg I've ever seen.

263
00:14:38,930 --> 00:14:40,430
That's what a snake egg looks like.

264
00:14:40,510 --> 00:14:42,670
But only the tail part of it's the snake.

265
00:14:42,990 --> 00:14:44,610
All the chickeny parts are the body.

266
00:14:44,770 --> 00:14:45,030
Uh-uh.

267
00:14:45,230 --> 00:14:46,770
The chickeny part's the tail.

268
00:14:46,770 --> 00:14:51,790
Hmm, this one's a different color than the others.

269
00:14:52,270 --> 00:14:53,030
You're right.

270
00:14:53,510 --> 00:14:54,970
Oh, that's the one Marsil got.

271
00:14:55,090 --> 00:14:55,930
It still has a head.

272
00:14:56,350 --> 00:14:56,650
Hmm.

273
00:14:57,970 --> 00:14:58,610
Mm-hmm.

274
00:14:59,470 --> 00:15:02,830
First, you chop the mandrakes up and stir-fry them for a bit.

275
00:15:03,250 --> 00:15:04,730
Crack an egg and whip it.

276
00:15:05,350 --> 00:15:08,930
Once it gets all fluffy in the pan, it's ready.

277
00:15:11,650 --> 00:15:13,970
Compared to a chicken egg, this is yellowier.

278
00:15:14,290 --> 00:15:16,410
This one's got the mandrake we picked in it.

279
00:15:16,410 --> 00:15:20,270
And this one's got the mandrake, the elf girl, picked using her big bat method.

280
00:15:20,750 --> 00:15:22,890
Sure, keep rubbing it in, why don't you?

281
00:15:23,150 --> 00:15:23,950
Now, now.

282
00:15:26,290 --> 00:15:26,770
Hmm.

283
00:15:27,330 --> 00:15:30,650
The one with Marsil's mandrake is less bitter and more velvety.

284
00:15:30,930 --> 00:15:31,210
Hmm.

285
00:15:31,510 --> 00:15:32,330
It is good.

286
00:15:32,670 --> 00:15:35,730
I guess it loses whatever toxins it has when it screams.

287
00:15:36,730 --> 00:15:40,690
The basic rule of cooking is the more effort you put in, the better the taste.

288
00:15:41,270 --> 00:15:45,190
I was so hell-bent on being efficient, I totally forgot that fact.

289
00:15:45,190 --> 00:15:47,170
My apologies, Marsil.

290
00:15:47,710 --> 00:15:49,710
You and that cookbook of yours were right.

291
00:15:50,010 --> 00:15:51,650
Hey, this is not a cookbook!

292
00:15:52,230 --> 00:15:54,470
As thanks for pointing out my mistake.

293
00:15:54,970 --> 00:15:57,450
I saved the tastiest parts for you.

294
00:15:57,650 --> 00:15:58,430
Oh, no!

295
00:16:00,190 --> 00:16:01,970
I don't want it!

296
00:16:01,970 --> 00:16:06,390
I'm sick and tired of eating these sticky monsters!

297
00:16:06,390 --> 00:16:12,230
The entrance to the secret path should be around here.

298
00:16:12,410 --> 00:16:13,270
I don't see it.

299
00:16:13,530 --> 00:16:14,830
Lyos, can I borrow your sword?

300
00:16:14,830 --> 00:16:23,330
Here it is.

301
00:16:27,330 --> 00:16:29,250
How the heck did he know?

302
00:16:30,010 --> 00:16:33,210
Chilchuk is a specialist in finding traps and picking locks.

303
00:16:33,970 --> 00:16:34,950
All right, people.

304
00:16:35,210 --> 00:16:38,850
There's tons of traps coming up, so don't make a move until I say so.

305
00:16:38,850 --> 00:16:46,470
One thing I hate more than anything is people getting in my way when I'm trying to work.

306
00:16:49,050 --> 00:16:49,610
Hmm.

307
00:16:56,070 --> 00:16:56,630
Stop!

308
00:16:56,630 --> 00:17:14,690
Only step where I just stepped.

309
00:17:14,690 --> 00:17:24,990
What are you doing?

310
00:17:25,290 --> 00:17:28,010
I clearly said only step where I just stepped.

311
00:17:28,390 --> 00:17:30,690
Why is your foot hanging over on this other tile?

312
00:17:31,010 --> 00:17:32,350
I don't nitpick details.

313
00:17:32,570 --> 00:17:33,790
But the details matter.

314
00:17:34,110 --> 00:17:36,470
If you don't watch your step, you'll kill someone.

315
00:17:36,710 --> 00:17:37,830
There are all kinds of traps.

316
00:17:37,970 --> 00:17:40,170
Some of them go off in tandem and some affect each other.

317
00:17:40,170 --> 00:17:43,410
If you trigger one of them, all my calculations go right out the window.

318
00:17:43,770 --> 00:17:46,250
Open your ears and step where I tell you to!

319
00:17:46,490 --> 00:17:46,810
Wait!

320
00:17:49,050 --> 00:17:49,490
Ah!

321
00:17:52,490 --> 00:17:52,930
Hmm.

322
00:17:56,930 --> 00:17:58,950
This room's got all sorts of traps.

323
00:17:59,250 --> 00:18:01,350
This one would crisp you up before you knew it.

324
00:18:01,510 --> 00:18:02,870
Get off that tile already!

325
00:18:03,310 --> 00:18:06,670
Fire traps spray oil and it won't stop till you move your dang foot!

326
00:18:06,810 --> 00:18:07,210
Oil?

327
00:18:07,330 --> 00:18:09,410
The last thing I need is a roasted dwarf!

328
00:18:09,410 --> 00:18:11,090
Roasted, huh?

329
00:18:11,730 --> 00:18:12,910
No, not roasted.

330
00:18:13,150 --> 00:18:13,350
Huh?

331
00:18:13,870 --> 00:18:14,270
Fried.

332
00:18:14,770 --> 00:18:16,290
No, deep fried.

333
00:18:17,230 --> 00:18:17,710
Kakiage!

334
00:18:19,110 --> 00:18:21,130
We're having tempura for lunch today.

335
00:18:21,910 --> 00:18:24,450
You know where the oil for this trap is stored?

336
00:18:24,970 --> 00:18:26,510
It's not used for cooking.

337
00:18:26,890 --> 00:18:28,230
All right, half-foot kid.

338
00:18:28,610 --> 00:18:30,290
You an expert in cooking oil now?

339
00:18:30,710 --> 00:18:32,490
I hate it when people call me kid.

340
00:18:32,750 --> 00:18:35,590
Vegetable oil is the most common, and I bet that's what it is.

341
00:18:35,950 --> 00:18:37,810
Either way, I just want to check it for myself.

342
00:18:37,810 --> 00:18:38,630
If not...

343
00:18:38,630 --> 00:18:40,090
No worries, I won't use it.

344
00:18:41,270 --> 00:18:43,250
All right, fine.

345
00:18:43,870 --> 00:18:48,450
But from now on, when we're dealing with traps, you have to swear you'll do exactly what I tell you!

346
00:18:48,650 --> 00:18:51,710
If you don't like following my orders, you can find the oil yourself!

347
00:18:51,710 --> 00:18:56,310
I swear I'll do what you say, and I'll help if you want.

348
00:18:56,450 --> 00:18:57,090
I don't!

349
00:18:57,870 --> 00:18:59,990
Look, we each have a specialty, don't we?

350
00:19:00,530 --> 00:19:01,350
You cook!

351
00:19:01,490 --> 00:19:03,270
I disarm traps and pick locks!

352
00:19:03,810 --> 00:19:04,830
Marcel Magix!

353
00:19:04,830 --> 00:19:07,830
I'd never tell you how to cook something, ever!

354
00:19:08,210 --> 00:19:11,870
So as a professional courtesy, leave me alone so I can do my job!

355
00:19:15,790 --> 00:19:16,830
I knew it.

356
00:19:17,150 --> 00:19:18,470
This is a treasure room.

357
00:19:18,770 --> 00:19:20,750
All those traps were designed to protect it.

358
00:19:23,050 --> 00:19:24,350
It's bolted down.

359
00:19:24,350 --> 00:19:27,510
I should check inside this one.

360
00:19:29,510 --> 00:19:30,790
Give me some space!

361
00:19:32,070 --> 00:19:34,790
He doesn't have to get all snippy about it.

362
00:19:35,170 --> 00:19:37,430
He knows if he screws up, we're dead.

363
00:19:38,370 --> 00:19:40,430
He's just stressing out about it is all.

364
00:19:42,970 --> 00:19:43,690
It's open.

365
00:19:44,410 --> 00:19:44,850
Great.

366
00:19:45,270 --> 00:19:47,090
So how are we gonna get the oil out of it?

367
00:19:47,230 --> 00:19:48,210
I have no idea.

368
00:19:48,210 --> 00:19:54,190
This looks like the spout, and this is the trigger, and if you pull on it, the oil comes out.

369
00:19:54,510 --> 00:19:55,150
I think.

370
00:19:55,710 --> 00:19:59,210
Okay, I'll hold the pot, and you pull the trigger thingy.

371
00:19:59,490 --> 00:19:59,830
Huh?

372
00:20:00,010 --> 00:20:00,970
That's crazy!

373
00:20:01,290 --> 00:20:02,690
It's filled with boiling oil!

374
00:20:03,290 --> 00:20:04,250
Don't worry.

375
00:20:04,610 --> 00:20:05,290
I'm worried!

376
00:20:08,510 --> 00:20:09,910
You asked for it!

377
00:20:12,830 --> 00:20:14,230
Hot, hot, hot, hot!

378
00:20:15,730 --> 00:20:17,390
That's what I was worried about.

379
00:20:18,210 --> 00:20:18,870
You okay?

380
00:20:19,230 --> 00:20:19,490
Ah!

381
00:20:19,830 --> 00:20:20,610
Your thumb!

382
00:20:20,950 --> 00:20:22,430
Hmm, 180 degrees.

383
00:20:22,970 --> 00:20:24,510
Perfect temperature for deep frying.

384
00:20:25,270 --> 00:20:27,610
So, you didn't feel that at all.

385
00:20:27,870 --> 00:20:28,570
That smell.

386
00:20:29,470 --> 00:20:30,250
That taste.

387
00:20:30,530 --> 00:20:31,630
This here is olive oil.

388
00:20:31,930 --> 00:20:32,510
You're kidding.

389
00:20:32,670 --> 00:20:36,150
There used to be tons of olive trees here, and olive oil's easy enough to make.

390
00:20:36,530 --> 00:20:38,310
Huh, figure someone would use it in a trap.

391
00:20:40,190 --> 00:20:41,350
Yeah, I guess.

392
00:20:41,350 --> 00:20:43,270
Now we can deep fry things.

393
00:20:43,870 --> 00:20:44,030
Oh!

394
00:20:44,490 --> 00:20:47,870
We can use that big blade from the trap in the ceiling to chop the meat.

395
00:20:48,210 --> 00:20:48,950
Hold on!

396
00:20:49,090 --> 00:20:51,250
We don't know if that trap will react the same way!

397
00:20:52,510 --> 00:20:56,990
Since I'm no good at dealing with traps, I'll leave trap stuff to the expert.

398
00:20:57,690 --> 00:20:59,310
Do I really have to do it?

399
00:20:59,690 --> 00:21:03,510
Cooking's my specialty, so you have to do whatever I say now.

400
00:21:03,970 --> 00:21:06,070
Those are the terms we agreed to.

401
00:21:06,070 --> 00:21:08,750
I never agreed to that.

402
00:21:17,550 --> 00:21:19,010
What the hell am I doing?

403
00:21:21,150 --> 00:21:22,910
The big bat's all cut up.

404
00:21:23,130 --> 00:21:23,510
Thanks.

405
00:21:25,190 --> 00:21:28,030
Max, get that fire trap going and heat this oil up.

406
00:21:28,030 --> 00:21:30,750
Go on, you got this.

407
00:21:34,410 --> 00:21:38,650
While he's doing that, I'll strip the skin off the meat, then chop the meat into chunks.

408
00:21:39,010 --> 00:21:42,610
After scoring the meat with a knife, I'll rub in some spices and let it rest.

409
00:21:43,150 --> 00:21:46,030
Now's as good a time as any to prepare the mandrake kakiage.

410
00:21:46,930 --> 00:21:49,750
Before you peel its skin off, you should remove its arms and legs.

411
00:21:50,030 --> 00:21:53,030
If they're all tangled, just separate them and they rip right off.

412
00:21:53,690 --> 00:21:58,410
Scrape all the skin off the body and where the arms and legs were, give those places a scrape, too.

413
00:21:58,990 --> 00:22:00,090
Next comes the batter.

414
00:22:00,630 --> 00:22:03,810
Mix a basilisk egg with water, then sift in a bit of flour,

415
00:22:04,250 --> 00:22:07,490
give it another mix-in and make damn sure there's no clumps,

416
00:22:07,770 --> 00:22:09,550
then add the chopped mandrake.

417
00:22:09,550 --> 00:22:11,430
How's that fire coming?

418
00:22:11,970 --> 00:22:13,090
I have no idea.

419
00:22:13,470 --> 00:22:16,310
It's not like I ever used a fire trap for cooking before.

420
00:22:18,370 --> 00:22:20,150
Oh, it's just about right.

421
00:22:25,330 --> 00:22:27,470
Once it's fried enough, it doesn't fall apart.

422
00:22:27,630 --> 00:22:28,030
Flip it.

423
00:22:28,290 --> 00:22:28,710
Got that?

424
00:22:29,130 --> 00:22:30,210
Yeah, I got it.

425
00:22:34,430 --> 00:22:35,310
Is it ready?

426
00:22:37,070 --> 00:22:38,410
Damn it, it wasn't ready.

427
00:22:39,550 --> 00:22:40,850
Maybe it needs more heat.

428
00:22:44,210 --> 00:22:45,670
I burned it.

429
00:22:45,970 --> 00:22:46,950
How's it coming along?

430
00:22:47,690 --> 00:22:48,130
Horribly.

431
00:22:48,310 --> 00:22:49,250
Can you just do this?

432
00:22:49,430 --> 00:22:51,170
Fire traps are your specialty.

433
00:22:51,330 --> 00:22:54,290
Traps are my specialty, but deep frying's a cooking thing.

434
00:22:54,870 --> 00:22:56,570
If the heat's too low, it's gooey.

435
00:22:57,010 --> 00:22:58,850
With too much heat, it burns right away.

436
00:22:59,550 --> 00:23:01,770
Then it's not your specialty after all.

437
00:23:02,570 --> 00:23:03,570
This batch looks okay.

438
00:23:06,070 --> 00:23:07,510
I like the color of that.

439
00:23:07,510 --> 00:23:10,170
And it's ready.

440
00:23:10,170 --> 00:23:15,910
I never thought eating deep-fried food was possible in a dungeon.

441
00:23:17,630 --> 00:23:19,270
This came out perfect.

442
00:23:19,670 --> 00:23:20,790
The heat was just right.

443
00:23:21,170 --> 00:23:23,650
When you deep fry, you do it quick at a specific temperature.

444
00:23:24,010 --> 00:23:25,530
That's how you get it nice and crispy.

445
00:23:26,130 --> 00:23:28,570
It's hard to get it right doing it over a campfire.

446
00:23:28,570 --> 00:23:31,210
Not that anyone should use a campfire.

447
00:23:31,910 --> 00:23:35,670
I've been on this floor a bunch and never knew about this useful room.

448
00:23:37,570 --> 00:23:38,550
It's dangerous!

449
00:23:38,910 --> 00:23:40,570
Don't ever come in here without me.

450
00:23:40,690 --> 00:23:41,090
Got it?

451
00:23:41,270 --> 00:23:42,570
You'll end up killing yourself.

452
00:23:43,090 --> 00:23:43,750
I hear you.

453
00:23:44,310 --> 00:23:47,190
And for the record, the way you handle traps is amazing.

454
00:23:47,670 --> 00:23:49,010
You sure know your stuff.

455
00:23:49,570 --> 00:23:52,850
The day we go our separate ways will be a sad day for me.

456
00:23:52,850 --> 00:23:56,090
I can't figure traps like you, that's for sure.

457
00:23:58,390 --> 00:23:59,890
It's not all that hard.

458
00:24:00,530 --> 00:24:04,110
When we get some downtime, I guess I can teach you a little about traps.

459
00:24:04,610 --> 00:24:05,130
Really?

460
00:24:05,630 --> 00:24:07,970
This could go bad in so many ways.

461
00:24:09,310 --> 00:24:12,930
But you did teach me a couple of your cooking skills.

462
00:24:12,930 --> 00:24:19,230
Meat cannot replace bread, and bread cannot replace meat.

463
00:24:19,570 --> 00:24:22,190
But combine them, and they can be quite delicious.

464
00:24:24,690 --> 00:24:27,330
So it is with meals and people.

465
00:24:28,050 --> 00:24:29,710
Delicious in Dungeon.

